WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.180
Your cells make a protein called p53 all day long.

2
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.600
Half of any new batch is destroyed within about twenty minutes.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.500
So why build a guardian, only to destroy it?

4
00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.580
Because a cell in danger cannot wait for a gene to be read and a protein to be built.

5
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:29.500
If the guardian is already there, and something holds it back, the cell only has to let go.

6
00:00:30.317 --> 00:00:37.217
p53 is a transcription factor, a protein that binds DNA and switches genes on.

7
00:00:37.957 --> 00:00:41.077
Its restraint is a protein called MDM2.

8
00:00:42.017 --> 00:00:45.877
MDM2 grips a short piece at the front end of p53.

9
00:00:46.557 --> 00:00:49.457
That grip covers the part that switches genes on.

10
00:00:50.137 --> 00:00:56.037
It also tags p53 with ubiquitin, a mark that sends it to the proteasome to be destroyed.

11
00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:00.910
Now DNA breaks across both strands.

12
00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:04.150
That switches on a kinase called ATM.

13
00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:09.710
ATM and the kinases beneath it add phosphates to MDM2.

14
00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:15.330
They also add phosphates to the stretch of p53 that MDM2 grips.

15
00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:19.330
The grip loosens, and destruction slows sharply.

16
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:25.313
So p53 piles up without any new gene being read.

17
00:01:25.933 --> 00:01:29.893
Free of its restraint, it switches on the gene for p21.

18
00:01:30.753 --> 00:01:34.173
That protein blocks the kinases that drive cell division.

19
00:01:34.853 --> 00:01:37.453
It switches on genes for DNA repair as well.

20
00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:42.333
If the damage is severe, it also switches on genes that kill the cell.

21
00:01:43.117 --> 00:01:48.277
Chemical threats use the same design, with a responder called Nrf2.

22
00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:54.697
Nrf2 switches on genes for protective enzymes, and the cell makes it constantly.

23
00:01:55.597 --> 00:02:01.277
A protein called Keap1 catches it and presents it to a ubiquitin ligase for destruction.

24
00:02:02.197 --> 00:02:04.737
Then a reactive chemical hits Keap1.

25
00:02:05.257 --> 00:02:11.237
On the current view, Keap1 then cannot present it, so new Nrf2 goes to the nucleus.

26
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:14.220
Put the two side by side.

27
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.980
In each, a responder is made all the time.

28
00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.120
Something holds it back, or destroys it.

29
00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.960
Stress removes the restraint, and the responder is already there.

30
00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:31.880
So keep this rule: a stress responder is made all the time and held back,

31
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.480
until stress removes the restraint.
